BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Reading and Signing: Childboss w/ Andrew Felsher
DTSTART:20261016T210000Z
DTEND:20261017T035959Z
DTSTAMP:20261010T152037Z
UID:a38c1cef22edcc7082a0d8fa66df5120662adbbd@corning-events
SEQUENCE:0
CATEGORIES:Literature
DESCRIPTION:Reading & Signing: Childboss\nJoin us for a reading and signin
 g event with Andrew Felsher author of Child Boss\, in conversation with Se
 an Sam!\nAbout the book: A young boy wants to be a star basketball player.
  But his family doesn’t have the money to nourish his desire to compete 
 against the best. This leads the boy to throw himself into work at twelve.
  As he metabolizes the pain of being a worker\, he confronts\, tests\, and
  reconfigures the troubling physics of power\, until the inescapable weigh
 t of capitalism is dragged into the foreground. Playful and unflinching\, 
 Child Boss obliterates the boundaries between boyhood and manhood\, boss a
 nd worker\, kindness and exploitation\, public and private life.\nAbout th
 e author: Andrew Felsher’s writing has appeared in EPOCH\, São Paulo Re
 view\, Heavy Feather Review\, Fiction Writers Review\, among other publica
 tions. He is the author of Notes from a Prison Cell (Bottlecap Press\, 202
 3)\, which has been translated into Portuguese\, and Child Boss (128 LIT\,
  forthcoming fall 2026). He lives in New York City.\nAbout the conversatio
 n partner: Sean Sam is a lecturer at Cornell University\, where he won the
  George Harmon Coxe Fiction Prize. His writing has appeared in Joyland\, T
 he Georgia Review\, Salt Hill\, Potomac Review\, The Malahat Review\, and 
 elsewhere. In 2026\, he was awarded a NYSCA/NYFA Artist Fellowship in nonf
 iction. He is the winner of Terrain.org’s 12th Annual Fiction contest\, 
 received an honorable mention in Zoetrope: All-Story’s Short Fiction Com
 petition\, and was a finalist for Poetry Northwest’s James Welch Prize. 
 He is a member of the Navajo Nation and is currently at work on a novel.\n
 Date: Fri\, 10/16/2026\nTime: 5:00pm - 6:30pm\nPlace:\nBuffalo Street Book
