BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Media\, Culture\, and Decolonization: Re-righting the Subaltern Hi
 stories of Ghana
DTSTART:20261023T093000Z
DTEND:20261024T035959Z
DTSTAMP:20261010T152037Z
UID:7bb82693a98fc66f8f658011c62153fc2561279d@corning-events
SEQUENCE:0
CATEGORIES:Literature,History & Heritage,BIPOC
DESCRIPTION:Media\, Culture\, and Decolonization: Re-righting the Subalter
 n Histories of Ghana\nJoin Wunpini Fatimata Mohammed in conversation with 
 Kemigisa Jackline and Rejoice Abutsa to celebrate the North American launc
 h of Media\, Culture\, and Decolonization. All are invited to be in conver
 sation with the author as she discusses the subaltern media and cultural h
 istories of Ghana.\nWUNPINI FATIMATA MOHAMMED is an assistant professor in
  the Department of Communication at Cornell University. Her research is pu
 blished in peer reviewed journals such as Communication Theory\, the Howar
 d Journal of Communications\, The International Journal of Communication\,
  Feminist Media Studies among others. She has won top paper awards at the 
 International Communication Association\, the Association for Education in
  Journalism and Mass Communication\, the International Association for Med
 ia and Communication Research and other academic conferences. She has work
 ed as a radio journalist in Ghana for several years and her writing has ap
 peared on Al Jazeera\, The Washington Post\, Global Voices\, Okay Africa\,
  and several Ghanaian media platforms.\nKemigisa Jackline is an independen
 t journalist\, researcher turned PhD student. Before choosing student life
 \, she worked as a research affiliate with the Center for Arts\, Design an
 d Social Research\, working with the team organizing the Black Planetary F
 uture Conversations. Her latest research report maps anti-Blackness in Eas
 tern Africa\, focusing on the role of narratives in reinforcing oppressive
  systems. As a journalist\, her work has appeared in Open Democracy\, Afri
 can Arguments\, BBC Africa\, Independent UK\, and others. As a researcher
 \, she contributes to the Northern Council for Global Cooperation’s Alte
 rnatives to Development Program that pursues decentering Western ways of b
 eing\, knowing\, and doing in international Development systems and practi
 ces.\nRejoice Abutsa is a Ph.D. candidate in the Department of Performing 
 and Media Arts at Cornell University. Her research threads interdisciplina
 ry knowledge and methods from Black Media Studies\, Gender & Women Studies
 \, Film Theory\, and Digital Studies. In her doctoral dissertation\, she r
 eorients\, reframes\, and recalibrates the institutional\, networked\, and
  cultural labor of women workers in Nollywood\, and broadly African cinema
 \, arguing that these women loom large and are crucial innovators in the p
 roduction and circulation of global black media.\n\nSource: FLXcalendar - 
 https://calendar.time.ly/o9b0uzpj/event/78480760/20261023053000
GEO:42.441218;-76.498352
LOCATION:Buffalo Street Books\, 215 North Cayuga Street\, Ithaca\, New Yor
 k\, 14850\, United States
STATUS:CONFIRMED
URL:https://calendar.time.ly/o9b0uzpj/event/78480760/20261023053000
END:VEVENT
