BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:FAUST at Sage Chapel - Live Score by Invincible Czars!
DTSTART:20261023T230000Z
DTEND:20261024T035959Z
DTSTAMP:20261010T152037Z
UID:d37a642b4dd430e5112b18891c97c91e45948c70@corning-events
SEQUENCE:0
CATEGORIES:Film,Music
DESCRIPTION:Event by Cornell Cinema and Wharton Studio Museum\nCornell Cin
 ema\nSage Chapel\, Ho Plaza\, Ithaca\, NY\nPublic\nPlease note that all ti
 ckets must be purchased online in advance of the event.\nCornell Cinema is
  thrilled to welcome the Invincible Czars back to Sage Chapel to celebrate
  100 years of the silent masterpiece FAUST (1926)!\nThe Invincible Czars w
 ill perform a brand new electrifying score that brings this classic film t
 o life for contemporary audiences by marrying new and old elements of clas
 sical music\, bossa nova\, and even a touch of heavy metal and incorporati
 ng a mix of instruments like flute\, violin and electric guitar. Their ann
 ual performance at Sage Chapel is a highlight of the spooky season in Itha
 ca!\nFaust—directed by the legendary F.W. Murnau—centers on an elderly
  alchemist named Faust (Gösta Ekman) who makes a pact with the demon Meph
 isto (Emil Jannings) to gain the power to protect his village from plague.
  Faust soon succumbs to temptation and asks Mephisto to restore his youth 
 and help him acquire all the hedonistic pleasures that earth can offer. Ev
 entually\, his love affair with a young woman named Gretchen (Camilla Horn
 ) leads to tragic and devastating consequences for them both.\nFaust is th
 e quintessential “deal with the devil” tale based on a centuries-old E
 uropean legend and Johann Wofgang Goethe’s 1808 play\, which is consider
 ed one of Germany’s greatest literary works. The play also inspired the 
 famous opera by Charles Guonod which director F.W. Murnau used as a map fo
 r his 1926 film. (Fun fact: Faust is the opera being presented in Gaston L
 eroux’s Phantom of the Opera\, which Cornell Cinema featured at Sage Cha
 pel last year.) Virtually every "sell your soul" type story from the film 
 Crossroads to songs like "The Devil Went Down To Georgia" owes a debt to F
 aust — whether their creators realize it or not!\nThe event will take pl
 ace at Sage Chapel\, Ho Plaza\, Ithaca\, New York. More information about 
 parking and directions is available on our website.\nSpecial event pricing
  applies and all tickets must be purchased online in advance. We expect a 
 sell-out crowd\, so please get your tickets early to avoid disappointment!
 \nThe Invincible Czars have made a name for themselves among horror and cl
 assic film lovers in the US and Canada with their original\, tastefully mo
 dern live soundtracks for silent films which they started creating for the
  original Alamo Drafthouse in their hometown of Austin\, TX in 2007\, thou
 gh they began making their whacky brand of cinematic rock in 2003. They ar
 e great at using music and sound to transform century-old movies into imme
 rsive and engaging experiences that allow audiences to approach\, apprecia
 te\, and enjoy the depth and innovation of classic silent film. Czars show
 s are a lot of fun with opportunities for audience participation and costu
 mery.\nCornell Cinema's annual Silent Film with Live Music at Sage Chapel 
 is cosponsored by the Wharton Studio Museum as part of Silent Movie Month 
 in Ithaca.\nPart of our "Altered States in Silent Cinema" series. Courtsey
 : Kino Lorber on behalf of Murnau Stiftung.\n\nSource: FLXcalendar - https
 ://calendar.time.ly/o9b0uzpj/event/78467618/20261023190000
STATUS:CONFIRMED
URL:https://calendar.time.ly/o9b0uzpj/event/78467618/20261023190000
END:VEVENT
